# Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array

## Problem Statement

Given a sorted integer array `arr`, remove the duplicates in-place such that each unique element appears only once. Return the number of unique elements.

The relative order of the elements must remain the same, and no extra array should be used.

---

# Approach 1: Using HashSet

### Idea

Store all unique elements in a `HashSet`. If an element is encountered for the first time, add it to the set and place it in the array at the next available position.

### Code Logic

```java
HashSet<Integer> unique = new HashSet<>();

int i = 0;

for (int number : arr) {
    if (!unique.contains(number)) {
        unique.add(number);
        arr[i] = number;
        i++;
    }
}
return i;
```

### Dry Run

Input:

```text
[1,1,2,2,3]
```

Process:

```text
1 -> Add
1 -> Skip
2 -> Add
2 -> Skip
3 -> Add
```

Array becomes:

```text
[1,2,3,2,3]
```

Return:

```text
3
```

### Time Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

### Space Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

### Drawback

Uses extra memory because of HashSet.

---

# Approach 2: Two Pointer (n Pointer)

### Idea

Since the array is sorted, duplicates are adjacent.

Maintain:

* `i` for traversal
* `n` for the position of the next unique element

Whenever a new element is found, place it at index `n` and increment `n`.

### Code Logic

```java
if (arr.length == 0) {
    return 0;
}

int n = 1;

for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {

    if (arr[i] != arr[n - 1]) {

        arr[n] = arr[i];
        n++;
    }
}

return n;
```

### Dry Run

Input:

```text
[1,1,2,2,3]
```

Process:

```text
1 -> Unique
1 -> Duplicate
2 -> Unique
2 -> Duplicate
3 -> Unique
```

Array becomes:

```text
[1,2,3,2,3]
```

Return:

```text
3
```

### Time Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

### Space Complexity

```text
O(1)
```

---

# Approach 3: Optimized Two Pointer (Most Common Solution)

### Idea

Keep one pointer `i` at the last unique element.

Use another pointer `j` to scan the array.

Whenever a new unique value is found:

1. Move `i` forward.
2. Copy the unique value to position `i`.

### Code Logic

```java
if (arr.length == 0)
    return 0;

int i = 0;

for (int j = 1; j < arr.length; j++) {

    if (arr[j] != arr[i]) {

        i++;
        arr[i] = arr[j];
    }
}

return i + 1;
```

### Dry Run

Input:

```text
[1,1,2,2,3]
```

Initial:

```text
i = 0
```

| j | arr[j] | arr[i] | Action       |
| - | ------ | ------ | ------------ |
| 1 | 1      | 1      | Skip         |
| 2 | 2      | 1      | i++, place 2 |
| 3 | 2      | 2      | Skip         |
| 4 | 3      | 2      | i++, place 3 |

Final Array:

```text
[1,2,3,2,3]
```

Unique Count:

```text
3
```

First 3 elements:

```text
[1,2,3]
```

---

# Logic Trick

The array is already sorted.

This means:

```text
All duplicates appear together.
```

Therefore, we only need to compare the current element with the last unique element.

```java
if (arr[j] != arr[i])
```

If they are different, we have found a new unique value.

---

# Complexity Comparison

| Approach              | Time | Space |
| --------------------- | ---- | ----- |
| HashSet               | O(n) | O(n)  |
| Two Pointer (n)       | O(n) | O(1)  |
| Optimized Two Pointer | O(n) | O(1)  |

---

# Recommended Approach

✅ Optimized Two Pointer Approach

Reason:

* Uses constant extra space.
* Modifies array in-place.
* Takes only one traversal.
* Accepted and expected by LeetCode interviews.
* Most efficient solution for a sorted array.
